    [Retrieved Chunk 1] [Source: tomato_leaf_mold.md] # Tomato - Leaf Mold

## Crop
Tomato

## Disease
Leaf Mold

## Cause
Commonly associated with the fungus Passalora fulva.

## Symptoms
- Pale green to yellow spots can appear on the upper surface of leaves.
- Olive-green to brown velvety growth may appear on the underside.
- Severe infections can cause leaves to curl and die.
- Disease is more common in protected or humid environments.

## Favorable Conditions
- High humidity strongly favors development.
- Poor air circulation increases risk.

#

[Retrieved Chunk 2] [Source: tomato_target_spot.md] # Tomato - Target Spot

## Crop
Tomato

## Disease
Target Spot

## Cause
The fungus Corynespora cassiicola.

## Symptoms
- Circular brown lesions may appear on leaves.
- Lesions can develop concentric rings resembling a target.
- Severe infections can cause leaf drop.
- Fruit may develop dark, sunken lesions.

## Favorable Conditions
- Warm and humid conditions.
- Extended leaf wetness.
- Dense foliage and poor air circulation.

## Prevention
- Improve air circulation.
- Avoid prolonged leaf wet

[Retrieved Chunk 3] [Source: tomato_late_blight.md] # Tomato - Late Blight

## Crop
Tomato

## Disease
Late Blight

## Cause
Primarily caused by Phytophthora infestans.

## Symptoms
- Irregular water-soaked green to brown lesions can develop on leaves.
- Lesions may expand rapidly.
- Under humid conditions, pale fungal-like growth may occur around lesions.
- Stems and fruit can also become affected.

## Favorable Conditions
- Cool to moderate temperatures.
- High humidity.
- Extended periods of leaf wetness.
